				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/manymeats" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2104082); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=2104082&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608238209" alt="ManyMeats" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=2104082)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/manymeats" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2104082); return false;" >ManyMeats</a></span> <span class="odate time_1553885976 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">29 Mar 2019 18:59</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4201184">
									

<p>This is a great first work, author!</p>
<p>I have a few issues with your tone and grammar in the work, but they do not detract from my enjoyment of this piece. I'd like to break one down one snippet as an example where you could have benefitted from more time editing.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The last page of each instance is blank. If an entry following the approved format is written in,</p>
</blockquote>
<p>You've put yourself in an awkward situation with the working here. You could have probably reworked this as &quot;If an entry is made in the approved format&quot; and left it at that. Somewhat consistently through your piece, there are times where you fall <em>just short</em> of outsmarting yourself and making language too cute. Please don't get me wrong, the tone is fine, but rather you were walking the line and I think you could tighten this up to great benefit.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>then related superstitions in native speakers of the corresponding language will take root, and the entry will become integrated into the culture of corresponding nationalities.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>First, that comma strikes me as not necessary. Second I think this should be reframed about people who are culturally members of a group, not just by language. There are a lot of places where a person might be born and raised under a language and under a nationality with exposure and tradition from an entirely different one. We live in a pretty global world today, comparatively. Just something to consider.</p>
<p>I echo Mortos's comment about the extra logs. While I feel you laid things out well logically, the log about the ink didn't add much except to serve as very long way to setup the Coca Cola entry as being a late add. I'd have like to see a Russian or Japanese entry instead of this ancillary tech piece.</p>
<p>This aside, a +1 from me and a work I enjoyed.</p>

							</div>

								
					
